consiSt("de",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Delaware","d":"Delaware’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. It hears appeals directly from the Court of Chancery and the Superior Court, usually in panels of three Justices (a quorum en banc is 5). It also takes certified questions from Delaware trial courts, federal courts, other states’ highest courts and the SEC, and it regulates the bar, including pro hac vice, in-house counsel certificates and discipline."},{"l":"EQUITY COURT","n":"Court of Chancery","d":"A court of equity with no juries. It hears all matters in equity unless an adequate remedy exists at law in another Delaware court, and it is the main forum for corporate, LLC, partnership, trust, fiduciary-duty, and stockholder litigation. It can also hear technology disputes by consent, and Magistrates in Chancery handle many matters, subject to review by a judge. It has its own rules, distinct from the Superior Court’s."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT (LAW)","n":"Superior Court","d":"The general-jurisdiction trial court for civil actions at law (with juries) and felonies, sitting in each county. Its Complex Commercial Litigation Division (CCLD) takes business cases with $1 million or more in controversy or an exclusive Delaware choice-of-court clause, under special rules (Rules 141–146, effective March 20, 2026). It also hears some appeals from lower courts and agencies."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Family Court; Court of Common Pleas; Justice of the Peace Court","d":"Family Court handles domestic relations and juvenile matters. The Court of Common Pleas handles civil claims within its statutory dollar limit, misdemeanors and some appeals. The Justice of the Peace Court handles small civil claims, landlord-tenant cases and minor criminal matters. Each has its own civil rules on courts.delaware.gov; they were not collected for this page."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Delaware adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025)","d":"Delaware replaced its narrow 1992 anti-SLAPP law with UPEPA, codified at 10 Del. C. ch. 60. The new law applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after September 15, 2025. Former §§ 8136–8138 were repealed the same day but still govern claims and motions in cases asserted before that date.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6001, 6013, 6014; 85 Del. Laws c. 217","v":true},{"t":"Scope: broad, with UPEPA’s usual exclusions","d":"UPEPA covers claims based on (1) communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative or other governmental proceeding; (2) communications on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (3) the exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition or association rights under the U.S. or Delaware Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nIt does not apply to claims against government units or officials acting officially, to government enforcement actions, or to claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services arising from a communication about those goods or services. It also excludes common-law fraud claims and claims under 6 Del. C. ch. 25, subchs. II or III. The commercial, fraud and ch. 25 exclusions do not apply to suits over consumer opinions, reviews, or ratings of businesses. The Act must be broadly construed.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6002, 6011","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, quick hearing","d":"A party may file a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading, or later for good cause. Filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery. The court may allow limited discovery that is needed on a specific issue.\nThe hearing must be held within 60 days after filing, and the ruling must come within 60 days after the hearing. The court decides on the pleadings and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. It dismisses with prejudice if the Act applies, no exception is shown, and either the responding party fails to make a prima facie case on each element or the claim fails under a Rule 12(b)(6) or summary-judgment standard.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6003–6008","v":true},{"t":"Fees mandatory; punitive damages possible","d":"A prevailing movant must be awarded court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees and litigation expenses related to the motion. The responding party gets fees only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely for delay. The court must also award punitive damages to a prevailing movant who proves the claim was brought or continued to harass, intimidate, punish or maliciously inhibit speech, petition or association. A voluntary dismissal does not defeat the fee motion, and a dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6007(b)–(c), 6010","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: 10 days, as of right, with a stay","d":"A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, but must file the appeal within 10 days after entry of the order, not the usual 30. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. A fee motion is not stayed.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6004(c), (e), 6009","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: same structure in every court, filed by Delaware counsel","d":"Each court admits out-of-state lawyers by its own rule: Superior Court Civil Rule 90.1, Court of Chancery Rule 170 and Supreme Court Rule 71. In each, a Delaware lawyer files a written motion, separately in each court, no later than the applicant’s first appearance, and certifies that the applicant is reputable and competent.\nThe applicant certifies good standing elsewhere, agrees to be bound by the Delaware Lawyers’ Rules of Professional Conduct and to have reviewed the Principles of Professionalism, and binds the applicant’s firm colleagues on the matter to the court’s rules. The applicant also consents to the clerk as agent for service (including for discipline), lists every Delaware case in the past 12 months, discloses any discipline, and lists all jurisdictions of general admission. The court may deny or revoke admission if the applicant is in effect practicing in Delaware without admission.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 90.1; Ct. Ch. R. 170; Supr. Ct. R. 71(a)–(g)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice assessment and annual renewal","d":"The assessment was $375 in 2015 and $400 in 2016 and has been indexed to inflation each year since. The current dollar figure is not stated in the rules, so check the court’s fee schedule. If the case continues into a later year, the assessment renews annually. In the Superior Court, it is payable January 1 and delinquent after February 1, with no proration. In the Supreme Court, it is payable February 1 and delinquent after March 1. Supreme Court Rule 71 adds a $50 late-filing assessment against Delaware counsel who miss the March 1 renewal notice.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 90.1(b)(vi); Supr. Ct. R. 71(b)(vi); Ct. Ch. R. 170(c)(vi)","v":true},{"t":"Delaware counsel stays on the hook","d":"Delaware counsel must appear, sign or receive service of all papers, and attend all court proceedings unless excused. Attendance at depositions is required only if ordered. In the Supreme Court, every paper must be signed by an active Delaware Bar member who maintains a bona fide Delaware office, and that lawyer or a firm colleague must attend all proceedings. A mail drop or answering service is not an office. In Chancery, letters from a lawyer must be signed by a Delaware lawyer, who may not simply forward an out-of-state lawyer’s letter.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 90.1; Ct. Ch. R. 7(d)(2), 170; Supr. Ct. R. 12(a), (d), 71(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is through File & ServeXpress, by Delaware lawyers","d":"The Superior Court, Court of Chancery and Supreme Court all use eFiling. Only a Delaware Bar member (or a self-represented party) may sign an eFiling, and a lawyer may let only firm staff or a retained service provider file on the lawyer’s behalf. Per-document technology surcharges are $1.25 in the Superior Court and Chancery and $1.50 in the Supreme Court. In Chancery, all civil filings must be e-filed by a Delaware-licensed attorney, and entities may not appear pro se.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 79.1; Ct. Ch. R. 5(j); Supr. Ct. R. 10.1(c)–(g); Ct. Ch. eFiling Best Practices § I","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and in-house counsel (2025 amendments)","d":"DLRPC 5.5(c) allows temporary practice in Delaware on the usual ABA grounds. An in-house lawyer with an office or other systematic and continuous presence in Delaware may practice for the employer under Rule 5.5(d)(1) only with a Supreme Court Rule 55.1 Certificate. The application is due within 30 days after starting employment, with a $250 fee and an employer affidavit. Both rules were amended effective July 14, 2025, in light of remote work. Comment [4] to DLRPC 5.5 warns that a presence may be systematic and continuous even without physical presence in Delaware.","c":"DLRPC 5.5(b)–(d) & cmt. [4]; Supr. Ct. R. 55.1(a)–(b); Order of July 14, 2025","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"DLRPC 1.2(c) permits a lawyer to limit the scope of a representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. No court rule on limited-scope appearances was found in the collected Superior Court, Chancery or Supreme Court rules. Withdrawal in the Supreme Court requires a court order.","c":"DLRPC 1.2(c); Supr. Ct. R. 12(b)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Delaware Lawyers’ Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"These rules follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, with Delaware additions (1.15A trust account overdraft notification, 1.17A dissolution of law firm) and deletions (2.2 and 3.10). Pro hac vice lawyers in every Delaware court must agree to be bound by them. The Supreme Court’s Principles of Professionalism for Delaware Lawyers are aspirational; they expressly may not be used as a basis for discipline or sanctions.","c":"DLRPC (table of rules); Supr. Ct. R. 71(b)(ii)","v":true},{"t":"No ABA 8.4(g) anti-harassment rule","d":"DLRPC 8.4 has only paragraphs (a)–(f). It does not adopt ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination in conduct related to the practice of law.","c":"DLRPC 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Advance fees must be refundable and held in trust","d":"DLRPC 1.5(f), which has no ABA counterpart, lets a lawyer require payment in advance only if the lawyer gives the client a written statement that the fee is refundable if not earned and states how it will be earned. Unearned fees must stay in the trust account, and the client gets a statement when funds are withdrawn. Fee-splitting between firms under 1.5(e) requires only written notice to the client, no objection, and a reasonable total fee. It does not require the ABA’s proportion-of-services or joint-responsibility condition.","c":"DLRPC 1.5(e)–(f)","v":true},{"t":"Detailed trust-account and bookkeeping rules","d":"DLRPC 1.15 goes well beyond the ABA rule. It applies to any lawyer in private practice in Delaware, even one whose office is elsewhere. Required records include receipts and disbursement journals, client subsidiary ledgers, and monthly reconciliations. Trust accounts must carry the title “Rule 1.15A Attorney Trust Account” (or a listed variant), and a separate operating account is mandatory. Records must be kept for 5 years, and the lawyer’s own funds in the trust account are capped at $2,000. Rule 1.15 was amended again effective November 20, 2025.","c":"DLRPC 1.15(a), (d); Order of Nov. 18, 2025","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality tracks the ABA rule","d":"DLRPC 1.6(b) allows disclosure on the ABA grounds, including to prevent, mitigate or rectify a client crime or fraud that used the lawyer’s services, and to detect conflicts when a lawyer changes firms. Paragraph (c) requires reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure or access. Disclosure is permissive, not mandatory.","c":"DLRPC 1.6","v":true}],"style":"State of Delaware","home":"https://courts.delaware.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Delaware has one appellate court and appeal times cannot be extended","d":"Civil appeals go straight to the Supreme Court. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, and the Supreme Court cannot enlarge the time to appeal or cross-appeal. Interlocutory appeals need a certification application in the trial court within 10 days of the order, plus a notice of appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i), 11(b), 42(c)(i), (d)(i); 10 Del. C. §§ 143, 145, 148"},{"t":"The 10-day anti-SLAPP appeal","d":"An order denying a UPEPA special motion must be appealed within 10 days after entry, not the usual 30.","c":"10 Del. C. § 6009"},{"t":"Short post-judgment windows in both trial courts","d":"In both the Superior Court and the Court of Chancery, reargument is due within 5 days after the decision. Motions for a new trial or to alter or amend the judgment are due within 10 days, not the federal 28. Opposition to reargument is due within 5 days.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(b), (d)–(e); Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)–(c)"},{"t":"20 days to answer, not 21","d":"An answer is due 20 days after service in both the Superior Court and Chancery. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due within 10 days.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 12(a); Ct. Ch. R. 12(a)"},{"t":"Verify Chancery pleadings","d":"Complaints, counterclaims, crossclaims, third-party complaints and their amendments must be verified by each filing party. The Register may reject a new-case filing that lacks verification.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 3(c); Ct. Ch. eFiling Best Practices § I(11)"},{"t":"Amend or stand: dismissal with prejudice","d":"In Chancery, and now in the Superior Court’s CCLD, a plaintiff facing a 12(b)(6) motion (or a 23.1 motion, in Chancery) must amend before the response is due. If the plaintiff stands on the complaint and loses, the dismissal is with prejudice as to that plaintiff.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 15(a)(5); Super. Ct. Civ. R. 143"},{"t":"Chancery filing deadline is 5:00 p.m.","d":"Except for new complaints, notices of appeal and papers in summary or expedited matters (due by midnight), Chancery filings made after 5:00 p.m. count as filed the next day.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 5(d)"},{"t":"Short Delaware limitations periods and the borrowing statute","d":"Contract, statutory and most other personal actions carry a 3-year period. Personal injury, wrongful death and wage claims carry 2 years. For a claim that arose outside Delaware, the shorter of Delaware’s period or the period where the claim arose applies, unless the claim originally belonged to a Delaware resident. A written contract involving at least $100,000 may set a period of up to 20 years.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 8106(a), (c), 8107, 8111, 8119, 8121"},{"t":"Pro hac vice renewal dates differ by court","d":"A pro hac vice assessment renews every January 1 in the Superior Court (delinquent after February 1) but every February 1 in the Supreme Court (delinquent after March 1, with a $50 late assessment). Delaware counsel must return each court’s renewal notice.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 90.1(b)(vi); Supr. Ct. R. 71(b)(vi)"},{"t":"Chancery cross-references still use old numbers","d":"Chancery rewrote and renumbered many rules in 2023–2026. For example, old Rule 15(aa) is now 15(a)(5), the e-filing rules 79.1 and 79.2 are gone, and the brief rule 171 moved to Rules 7 and 10. Yet the 2023 eFiling guide and some internal cross-references (such as Rule 144(f) citing ‘Rule 59(f)’) still use the old numbers. Check the current text.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 7, 10, 15(a)(5), 59(c), 144(f), 171"},{"t":"Modified comparative negligence (not greater than)","d":"A plaintiff whose negligence is not greater than the combined negligence of the defendants may recover, but damages are reduced in proportion to the plaintiff’s share of fault.","c":"10 Del. C. § 8132"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge","d":"None of the collected Superior Court, Chancery or Supreme Court rules gives a party a right to strike or substitute a judge without cause. The Superior Court’s President Judge, as Assignment Judge, may designate a judge for all proceedings in a case on motion for good cause. In the Supreme Court, a disqualified Justice is replaced by the next available Justice, and retired Justices or constitutional judges may be assigned to complete a quorum.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 40; Supr. Ct. R. 2(a), 4","v":true},{"t":"Recusal under the Judges’ Code of Judicial Conduct","d":"Disqualification is governed by Rule 2.11 of the Delaware Judges’ Code of Judicial Conduct. The Code itself was not collected. A July 14, 2025 order amended Rule 2.11(A)(4). It requires disqualification where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including where the judge was associated within the preceding year with a firm or lawyer acting as counsel in the proceeding. A new comment says a former government lawyer should step aside if impartiality might reasonably be questioned because of that association.","c":"Del. Judges’ Code of Judicial Conduct R. 2.11(A)(4) (as amended July 14, 2025)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"State constitutional speech protection not checked","d":"UPEPA protects speech, press, assembly, petition and association rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Delaware’s Constitution,’ so the Delaware Constitution matters to its scope. The text of the Delaware Constitution (art. I, § 5) was not collected, and whether Delaware courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not verified.","c":"10 Del. C. § 6002(b)(3); Del. Const. art. I, § 5 (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Choosing the right court: Chancery (equity) vs. Superior (law)","d":"The Court of Chancery has jurisdiction over all matters in equity but not over any matter where a sufficient remedy exists at law or by statute in another Delaware court. Damages-only contract and tort claims therefore belong in the Superior Court, where juries are available, unless equity jurisdiction attaches. Chancery may also hear technology disputes by consent, with no jury or punitive damages; monetary-only disputes need at least $1 million in controversy.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 341, 342, 346; Ct. Ch. R. 91–93","v":true},{"t":"Superior Court Complex Commercial Litigation Division (2026 rules)","d":"Effective March 20, 2026, CCLD cases follow Superior Court Civil Rules 141–146. These rules borrow several Chancery features: a confidential-filing regime with tight public-version deadlines, an amend-or-stand rule on 12(b)(6) motions, and word limits (500 words for a motion with a brief; 3,000 for other motions and oppositions; 2,000 for replies). Discovery motions also require Delaware counsel on both sides to have actually spoken before filing. A case is designated by coding the Case Information Statement “CCLD.”","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 141–146 (Order of Mar. 13, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Superior Court: litigation-funding discovery (Sept. 30, 2026)","d":"New Superior Court Civil Rule 26(b)(3) lets a party discover only four things about a litigation-funding agreement: whether it exists, who the parties are, how it meets the rule’s definition, and what aspects of the litigation the funder may control. The agreement itself and the funder’s work product are generally protected, and the agreement is inadmissible at trial.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 26(b)(3) (Order of Sept. 30, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Verified pleadings","d":"Complaints, counterclaims, crossclaims, third-party complaints, and any amendments or supplements to them must be verified under oath or by affirmation by each filing party, and an authorized person must verify for an entity. The Register’s eFiling guidance treats a missing notarized verification from each plaintiff as grounds to reject the new-case filing, and requires the verification to be uploaded separately as a “Verification to Complaint”; an unsworn declaration under 10 Del. C. § 3927 or § 5354 may be used instead.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 3(c); eFiling Best Practices § I(11), § VI(8), § VI(29)","v":true},{"t":"Supplemental information sheet and deposits","d":"Every complaint must be accompanied by a completed supplemental information sheet in the Court’s form. The eFiling guidance says the form should be one page, uploaded separately, with every section completed or marked N/A; no new form is needed for an amended complaint. Filing also requires an initial nonrefundable deposit (with listed fiduciary-type exceptions), a per-filing technology surcharge, and a supplemental court security fee for each initial civil filing.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 3(b), 3(d), 3(g); eFiling Best Practices § VI(27)","v":true},{"t":"Expedited and summary proceedings","d":"The rules contain no stand-alone “motion to expedite” rule, but expedition changes several deadlines. In a summary proceeding or an action the Court has ordered expedited, papers may be filed and served until midnight (rather than 5:00 p.m.), the 30-day automatic stay of judgments does not apply, and exceptions to a Magistrate’s report must be noticed within three days. The eFiling guidance allows a proposed order on a motion to expedite (or a TRO) to be filed with a new case; all other proposed orders must wait until a judge is assigned.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 5(d)(2), 62(a), 144(d)(2); eFiling Best Practices § VI(20)","v":true},{"t":"Confidential filings and public versions","d":"A paper may be filed confidentially only if it contains Confidential Information, and a filer must file confidentially if it believes another person would contend the paper contains Confidential Information. After a confidential filing of an original complaint, notice to interested persons is due by 3:00 p.m. the next day, and redaction designations are due by 3:00 p.m. on the third day. For other filings, notice is due by 3:00 p.m. two days after filing, and designations are due by 3:00 p.m. on the fifth day. The public version is due the day after the designation deadline. Confidential treatment expires three years after final disposition unless extended, and the three extra days for mail service do not apply.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 5.1(b)–(f), (h), (i)","v":true},{"t":"Motions, briefs, and letters have word limits","d":"Former Rule 171 was repealed in 2023 and its content moved to Rules 7 and 10. A motion supported by a brief may not exceed 500 words. A motion without a brief, and the opposition to it, may not exceed 3,000 words each, and the reply may not exceed 2,000. Opening and answering briefs are capped at 14,000 words, reply briefs at 8,000, and letters at 1,000. The signature block must state the applicable word limit and the word count.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 7(b)(3), 7(c)(3), 7(d)(3), 10(d), 171","v":true},{"t":"Format, letters, and compendia","d":"Filings must use Times New Roman 14-point (13-point footnotes), Century 12-point, or Century Schoolbook 12-point, with at least double line spacing and 1-inch margins. Citations follow the Blue Book as modified by Delaware’s Yellow Book. A letter may not seek relief without the Court’s authorization, and any letter from a lawyer must be signed by a Delaware lawyer. A party may submit a compendium of authorities, and briefing is limited to Rule 12, 23, 23.1, 41(b), 56, and 65 motions, settlements and fee applications in representative actions, and pre-trial and post-trial briefs unless the Court orders otherwise.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 7(c)(1), 7(d), 7(e), 10(b)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Amend-or-stand on motions to dismiss","d":"Former Rule 15(aa) is now Rule 15(a)(5). A plaintiff facing a Rule 12(b)(6) or 23.1 motion to dismiss must amend, or seek leave to amend, before its response to the motion is due, or within 30 days after a transfer from another court. If the plaintiff instead stands on the complaint and loses, the dismissal is with prejudice as to the named plaintiff, unless the Court finds good cause to dismiss without prejudice. An amended pleading must be filed with a document showing how it differs from the earlier pleading.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 15(a)(3), 15(a)(5)","v":true},{"t":"Class actions: affidavits, settlements, fees","d":"A proposed representative party must file an affidavit within 10 days after filing a complaint, a motion to intervene, or a motion for appointment, swearing that the person has received no compensation for serving beyond what the rule permits. A class action may be dismissed or settled only with Court approval after notice, with a further representative affidavit and the definitive settlement agreement. Approval of a settlement that binds the class requires a hearing and a reasonableness finding. Every counsel who will share in a fee award must submit an affidavit documenting fees and expenses, and residual funds may go to the Combined Campaign for Justice or a similar organization.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 23(aa), 23(f), 23(g)","v":true},{"t":"Derivative actions (Rule 23.1)","d":"A derivative complaint must state with particularity any effort to obtain the desired action from the entity and the reasons for not obtaining it or not making the effort, which is the rule-based hook for demand futility. It must also plead facts supporting standing under the law governing the entity. The derivative plaintiff must file a no-compensation affidavit within 10 days and must be represented by counsel. If the sole plaintiff is inadequate, the action is dismissed without prejudice, but an alternative plaintiff may move to intervene within 60 days. Dismissal or settlement requires Court approval, and every counsel sharing in a fee award must file a fee affidavit.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 23.1(a)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Voluntary dismissal limits","d":"A plaintiff may dismiss by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or summary-judgment motion. That right is subject to Rules 23 and 23.1, and a notice of dismissal is not effective when the complaint faces a motion to dismiss and the plaintiff has filed an answering brief rather than amending. A case with no action for 1 year may be dismissed after reasonable notice.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 41(a)(1), 41(e)","v":true},{"t":"Expense and fee awards","d":"Rule 88 was rewritten effective June 1, 2026. A request for expenses, including attorney’s fees, may be made in a paper or by separate motion that quantifies the amount. A person moving to quantify an award must file an affidavit summarizing the work and, for each timekeeper, giving the title, hourly rate, hours, and amount. The affidavit must also summarize out-of-pocket costs by category and state that the applicant incurred the amount sought. Rule 88 does not apply to petitions under Rule 192.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 88","v":true},{"t":"Magistrates in Chancery and exceptions","d":"The Chancellor may assign an action or dispute to a Magistrate in Chancery, whose rulings are “Reports” subject to de novo review by a Reviewing Judge. Outside the civil miscellaneous docket, exceptions are taken only after the Final Report. In non-expedited matters, the notice of exceptions is due within 11 days, the opening brief 20 days after that, the answering brief 20 days later, and the reply 15 days later. In summary or expedited matters, notice is due within 3 days and a proposed briefing schedule within 5 days. If no exceptions are filed, the Report is adopted, and a missed opening brief withdraws the exceptions.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 144(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"No jury; equity-court structure","d":"The jury-trial rules (Rules 38, 39, and 47–51) are omitted, as are Rule 52 and Rule 68 (offer of judgment), so trials are to the Court. A preliminary injunction or TRO request must be supported by an affidavit or a verified pleading. A TRO issued without notice expires within no more than 10 days unless extended for up to 30 more days, and the movant must post security.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 38, 39, 47–52, 65(a)–(c), 68","v":true},{"t":"2023–2026 rule rewrites and renumbering","d":"The Court has rewritten its rules in tranches. Effective September 25, 2023, it amended Rules 7, 10, and 17–25, including 23, 23.1, and 23.2, and repealed Rule 171 with its content moving to Rules 7 and 10. Effective June 14, 2024 (order of May 31, 2024), it amended Rules 1–6, 5.1, 8–15, 23, 23.1, and 79 and omitted Rules 79.1 and 79.2, with e-filing now in Rules 5(c)–(e) and (j); the old Rule 15(aa) became 15(a)(5), Rule 5(aa) became 5(i), and Rule 4(dc) became 4(e). Rules 143–144 were amended effective December 9, 2024 and again (with guardianship rules 175 through 180-D, including new 175-A and 175-B) effective September 30, 2025. Effective June 1, 2026 (order of May 18, 2026), it amended Rules 46, 54–65.1, 67, 69–72, 77, 78, 81–83, 85, 86, and 88 and omitted Rule 87, and a May 21, 2026 order amended Rules 178A and 180-B the same day.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 1–25, 79–79.2, 86, 171 (History notes); May 21, 2026 Order (Rules 178A, 180-B)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Rules of the Supreme Court of the State of Delaware (consolidated text, courts.delaware.gov download id 174928, reflecting the July 14 and Nov. 18, 2025 amendment orders); 10 Del. C. §§ 141–150 and ch. 60 (delcode.delaware.gov, fetched Nov. 10, 2026); Superior Court Civil Rules 54–62 (consolidation id 304488); Court of Chancery Rules 54–72 and 144 (consolidation id 160908, amendments effective June 1, 2026). Official texts; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Delaware: one appellate court, short fuses, no extensions","d":"Delaware has no intermediate appellate court. Final judgments of the Court of Chancery and the Superior Court go directly to the Supreme Court of Delaware, which hears most cases in panels of three Justices. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, and the Supreme Court cannot extend that time. Interlocutory appeals require a certification application in the trial court within 10 days, and the Supreme Court decides in its discretion whether to accept them. Briefing is fast, extensions are discouraged, and filings must be signed by a Delaware lawyer with a Delaware office.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 4, 6, 11(b), 12(a), 42; 10 Del. C. §§ 145, 148"},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days from docket entry","d":"In a civil case, file the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after the judgment, order or decree is entered on the docket. The statutes measure the time from the date of the judgment of the Court of Chancery or Superior Court. The time to appeal or cross-appeal cannot be enlarged. The notice must name the court and judge below, the parties and their counsel, and the order appealed from, and must attach that order. If the appeal is filed more than 30 days after entry, the notice must state the factual and legal grounds for treating the appeal time as tolled.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i), 7(c), 11(b); 10 Del. C. §§ 145, 148"},{"t":"Filing fee, disclosure statement, e-filing","d":"The filing fee is $500 plus a $10 Court Security Assessment, and it is nonrefundable. Each party must file a Disclosure of Corporate Affiliations and Financial Interest (Form P) within 15 days after notice that the appeal has been docketed. Appeals of cases e-filed below are e-filed in the Supreme Court, with a $1.50 per-document technology surcharge, and an e-filing counts as the lawyer’s signature.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 7(g), 10.1(b)–(f), 20(a)"},{"t":"Delaware counsel must sign and attend","d":"Except for self-represented parties, every paper must be signed by an active Delaware Bar member who keeps a bona fide office in Delaware, and that lawyer or a firm colleague must attend all proceedings. Out-of-state counsel must be admitted pro hac vice under Rule 71; the motion is filed by Delaware counsel no later than the first appearance.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 12(a), (d), 71"},{"t":"Only issues raised below","d":"Only questions fairly presented to the trial court may be raised on appeal, although the Court may consider others when the interests of justice require.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 8"}]],["Tolling motions in the trial court",[{"t":"Rule 59 motions delay finality; Rule 60 motions do not","d":"In Chancery, a timely motion for a new trial, to alter or amend, or for reargument makes the judgment not final until the Court rules. A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality in either trial court. The Supreme Court rules do not themselves list tolling motions; Rule 7(c)(3) only requires a late notice of appeal to explain why the time was tolled. File any Rule 59 motion within its own short deadline.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 59(d), 60(c)(2); Super. Ct. Civ. R. 60(b); Supr. Ct. R. 7(c)(3)"},{"t":"Trial-court deadlines are short","d":"In the Superior Court, a new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are due within 10 days after entry of judgment, and reargument within 5 days after the decision is filed. In Chancery, a new-trial or alter-or-amend motion is due within 10 days, and reargument or clarification within 5 days after the ruling, with no reply. Neither court can extend these times except as Rule 59 allows.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 6(b), 59(b), (d), (e); Ct. Ch. R. 6(b)(2), 59(a)–(c)"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"15 days after the first notice, or 30 days after judgment, whichever is later","d":"Any party may cross-appeal from any appealable judgment or order. In a civil case, a cross-appeal is timely if filed within 15 days after the first notice of appeal was filed, or within 30 days after entry of the judgment, whichever is later. In the briefing, the party who first filed a notice of appeal is treated as the appellant.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(b)(i), 7(b), 15(a)(iv); 10 Del. C. § 149"}]],["Record and briefing",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"If no transcript is designated, the opening brief and appendix are due 45 days after the notice of appeal; otherwise they are due 30 days after the record is filed. The answering brief is due 30 days after service of the opening brief. The reply is due 15 days later, or 30 days if it also answers a cross-appeal; the cross-appellee’s reply on the cross-appeal is due 10 days after that. Other argumentative filings require leave; a letter citing new authority may not contain argument.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)"},{"t":"Extensions are disfavored and strictly limited","d":"A motion to extend must be docketed at least 5 days before the due date and state the opponent’s position. The Clerk may grant an unopposed first extension of 3 days. Further extensions require exceptional circumstances (serious illness, a death in the immediate family, an act of God, an emergency, or similar) and are capped at 15 days total for opening and answering briefs and 10 days for replies. Late briefs will not be docketed without leave, and a missing opening brief can lead to dismissal. A lawyer who files more than two out-of-time extension motions in six months faces discipline under Rule 33.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(b)"},{"t":"Word limits and format","d":"Opening and answering briefs are limited to 10,000 words and replies to 5,500. With a cross-appeal, the appellee’s combined answering/opening brief may run 14,000 words, the appellant’s reply/answering brief 10,000, and the cross-appeal reply 5,500. A certificate of compliance stating the word count is required. Text, including footnotes, must be in Times New Roman 14-point, double-spaced, with 1-inch margins; footnotes may not carry argument. Unrepresented parties without word processing are limited to 35 and 20 pages.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 13(a), 14(d)"},{"t":"Argument","d":"Oral argument is held only in cases the Court designates; other cases are submitted on the briefs. Each side gets 20 minutes before a panel and 25 minutes before the Court en banc. A copy of any case decided after briefing must be sent to the Court and opponents at least 48 hours before argument.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 16(a), (f), (g)"}]],["Rehearing and further review",[{"t":"Reargument: 15 days","d":"A motion for reargument may be filed within 15 days after the opinion or order is filed. It must state its grounds succinctly and carry a good-faith certificate. No answer is allowed unless the Court requests one, and there is no oral argument. No reargument is available when the mandate issues forthwith, or from orders under Rules 41 and 42.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 18"},{"t":"Rehearing en banc after a panel decision","d":"A unanimous three-Justice panel decision is the decision of the Court. A motion for rehearing en banc may be filed within 15 days, unless the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith. Grounds are a question of exceptional importance, the need for uniformity, or a prior decision that should be reconsidered. A non-unanimous panel automatically sends the case to the Court en banc.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 4(d)–(f)"},{"t":"No further state review; mandate","d":"The Supreme Court is the court of last resort, so there is no further state review. The mandate issues when the time for reargument or rehearing expires, or after the Court rules on such a motion.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 19(a)"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"Delaware trial courts, federal courts, other states’ highest courts and the SEC may certify questions of law before final judgment. The Court accepts a question only for important and urgent reasons and only if the material facts are undisputed.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 41"}]],["Interlocutory appeals",[{"t":"Two-step certification under Rule 42","d":"First, apply to the trial court for certification within 10 days after entry of the order. The opponent responds within 10 days, and the trial court rules within 10 days after the response, or within 20 days of the application if there is no response. Second, file the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days after entry of the order, even if the trial court has not yet ruled, followed by a supplementary notice within 10 days after the trial court’s time expires.\nThe order must decide a substantial issue of material importance, and the Rule 42(b)(iii) factors must favor review; if the balance is uncertain, the trial court should refuse. The Supreme Court decides acceptance in its discretion. There is no automatic stay, and failing to seek interlocutory review does not waive the issue on final appeal.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 42(b)–(f); 10 Del. C. §§ 143, 144"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denial: appeal as of right in 10 days","d":"An order denying a UPEPA special motion in whole or in part is appealable as of right by the movant, but the appeal must be filed within 10 days after the order is entered. All proceedings are stayed while that appeal is pending.","c":"10 Del. C. §§ 6004(c), 6009"},{"t":"Partial judgments","d":"A judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is appealable only if the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 54(b); Super. Ct. Civ. R. 54(b)"}]],["Stays pending appeal",[{"t":"Automatic stays below are short","d":"Execution is stayed for 10 days after judgment in the Superior Court. In Chancery, it is stayed for 30 days, except for expedited or summary proceedings, equitable relief and Rule 54(b) judgments. A longer stay pending appeal requires security under Article IV, § 24 of the Delaware Constitution, with applications processed under Supreme Court Rule 32.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 62; Ct. Ch. R. 62(a)–(e); Supr. Ct. R. 32, 42(e)"}]],["Appeals from the Court of Chancery",[{"t":"What is a final, appealable judgment","d":"A “judgment” includes any order from which an appeal lies, and except under Rule 54(b) a final order is the judgment. A partial judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay. Otherwise, such an order can be revised at any time before final judgment.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 54(a)–(b), 58"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal and certified questions","d":"A party may move in the Court of Chancery to certify a question of law or an interlocutory appeal to the Delaware Supreme Court. The motion must comply with the applicable Supreme Court rules (Supreme Court Rule 42 for interlocutory appeals) and include a proposed form of order. The appeal itself is filed in the Supreme Court, and the eFiling guide notes a $500 Register appeal fee that must be paid before the record is transmitted.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 72(b); eFiling Best Practices § VI(1)"},{"t":"Post-judgment motions","d":"A motion for a new trial or other post-trial action, or to alter or amend the judgment, is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. Reargument or clarification must be sought within five days after the ruling, with any opposition due within five days of the motion. A timely Rule 59 motion keeps the judgment from being final until the Court rules on it, while a Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)(3), 59(b), 59(c), 59(d), 60(c)(2)"},{"t":"Stays pending appeal","d":"Execution on a judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, except in expedited or summary proceedings, orders awarding equitable relief, and Rule 54(b) judgments. A money judgment is stayed once the Court approves a bond or other security. Applications for a stay or injunction pending appeal must comply with the Supreme Court Rules and, for security, Article IV, § 24 of the Delaware Constitution.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 62(a)–(e)"},{"t":"Appeal timing traps","d":"Not receiving a copy of an order does not extend the time to appeal. Once an appeal is docketed, clerical mistakes may be corrected only with the Supreme Court’s leave.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 77(d)(2), 60(a)"},{"t":"Magistrate decisions and appeal waivers","d":"The parties may stipulate under 10 Del. C. § 350 to final adjudication by a Magistrate in Chancery, which is appealed like a decision of the Chancellor or a Vice Chancellor. Under § 351, they may instead stipulate that the Magistrate’s Report is final and not subject to appeal. Otherwise, Reports become orders of the Court only through adoption or the exceptions process.","c":"Ct. Ch. R. 144(c)(2), 144(g)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, final judgment)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment on the docket.\nCannot be enlarged (Supr. Ct. R. 11(b)).","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i); 10 Del. C. §§ 145, 148","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment on the docket)","note":"Cannot be enlarged (Supr. Ct. R. 11(b))."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or 30 days from judgment, whichever is later).","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(b)(i); 10 Del. C. § 149","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Challenge to a Delaware Rapid Arbitration Act final award","d":"15 days from issuance of the final award.\nCross-challenge: 7 days after the first notice is served, or 15 days after the award, whichever is later.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(ii)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the final award)","note":"Cross-challenge: 7 days after the first notice is served, or 15 days after the award, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Application to certify an interlocutory appeal (trial court)","d":"10 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nThe trial court may allow longer for good cause.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 42(c)(i)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":"The trial court may allow longer for good cause."}},{"t":"Notice of interlocutory appeal (Supreme Court)","d":"30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nFile even if the trial court has not ruled on certification.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 42(d)(i); 10 Del. C. § 143","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":"File even if the trial court has not ruled on certification."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) motion","d":"10 days from entry of the order.\nApplies to actions filed or claims asserted on or after Sept. 15, 2025.","c":"10 Del. C. § 6009","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Applies to actions filed or claims asserted on or after Sept. 15, 2025."}},{"t":"Disclosure of corporate affiliations (Form P)","d":"15 days from notice of docketing of the appeal.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 7(g)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (notice of docketing of the appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Opening brief and appendix","d":"30 days from filing of the record (or 45 days after the notice of appeal if no transcript is designated).","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(i)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":""}},{"t":"Answering brief","d":"30 days from service of the opening brief.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(ii)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opening brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"15 days from service of the answering brief.\n30 days if the reply also answers a cross-appeal; cross-appellee’s reply 10 days after that.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(iii)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answering brief)","note":"30 days if the reply also answers a cross-appeal; cross-appellee’s reply 10 days after that."}},{"t":"Motion to extend a briefing deadline","d":"5 days from before the due date (must be docketed at least 5 days in advance).\nCounted backward from the due date.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 15(b)(i)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (before the due date)","note":"Counted backward from the due date."}},{"t":"Motion for reargument (Supreme Court)","d":"15 days from filing of the opinion or order.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 18","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion or order)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for rehearing en banc","d":"15 days from filing of the panel’s opinion or order.\nNot available if the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith.","c":"Supr. Ct. R. 4(f)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the panel’s opinion or order)","note":"Not available if the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith."}},{"t":"Reargument in the trial court (Superior Court or Chancery)","d":"5 days from filing of the decision.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(e); Ct. Ch. R. 59(c)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the decision)","note":""}},{"t":"New trial or motion to alter or amend (trial court)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nNot the federal 28 days.","c":"Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(b), (d); Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)–(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Not the federal 28 days."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (civil, final judgment)",30,"entry of the judgment on the docket","Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(i); 10 Del. C. §§ 145, 148","Cannot be enlarged (Supr. Ct. R. 11(b))."],["Cross-appeal",15,"filing of the first notice of appeal (or 30 days from judgment, whichever is later)","Supr. Ct. R. 6(b)(i); 10 Del. C. § 149",""],["Challenge to a Delaware Rapid Arbitration Act final award",15,"issuance of the final award","Supr. Ct. R. 6(a)(ii)","Cross-challenge: 7 days after the first notice is served, or 15 days after the award, whichever is later."],["Application to certify an interlocutory appeal (trial court)",10,"entry of the interlocutory order","Supr. Ct. R. 42(c)(i)","The trial court may allow longer for good cause."],["Notice of interlocutory appeal (Supreme Court)",30,"entry of the interlocutory order","Supr. Ct. R. 42(d)(i); 10 Del. C. § 143","File even if the trial court has not ruled on certification."],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) motion",10,"entry of the order","10 Del. C. § 6009","Applies to actions filed or claims asserted on or after Sept. 15, 2025."],["Disclosure of corporate affiliations (Form P)",15,"notice of docketing of the appeal","Supr. Ct. R. 7(g)",""],["Opening brief and appendix",30,"filing of the record (or 45 days after the notice of appeal if no transcript is designated)","Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(i)",""],["Answering brief",30,"service of the opening brief","Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(ii)",""],["Reply brief",15,"service of the answering brief","Supr. Ct. R. 15(a)(iii)","30 days if the reply also answers a cross-appeal; cross-appellee’s reply 10 days after that."],["Motion to extend a briefing deadline",5,"before the due date (must be docketed at least 5 days in advance)","Supr. Ct. R. 15(b)(i)","Counted backward from the due date."],["Motion for reargument (Supreme Court)",15,"filing of the opinion or order","Supr. Ct. R. 18",""],["Motion for rehearing en banc",15,"filing of the panel’s opinion or order","Supr. Ct. R. 4(f)","Not available if the panel ordered the mandate to issue forthwith."],["Reargument in the trial court (Superior Court or Chancery)",5,"filing of the decision","Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(e); Ct. Ch. R. 59(c)",""],["New trial or motion to alter or amend (trial court)",10,"entry of judgment","Super. Ct. Civ. R. 59(b), (d); Ct. Ch. R. 59(a)–(b)","Not the federal 28 days."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://courts.delaware.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Rules of Civil Procedure for the Superior Court of the State of Delaware","rulesSrc":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope and purpose of Rules","s":"The Rules govern procedure in the Superior Court of Delaware, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed, administered, and employed by the Court and the parties to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every proceeding.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action starts by filing with the Prothonotary a complaint plus a praecipe directing the Prothonotary to issue the writ, with copies for each defendant and a Case Information Statement (CIS); an objection to the Related Cases listed on another party’s CIS is due within 10 days after the last responsive pleading, with responses within 5 days, and the Civil Administrative Judge may reassign the case with the President Judge’s approval. A $200 nonrefundable deposit for costs is required before filing (another $200 for a third-party complaint; at least $750 if a special jury is requested); de novo appeals start by praecipe within the statutory time or, if none, 15 days after the final judgment, with the record due within 10 days of the praecipe; nonresident plaintiffs may be ordered to post security for costs on 5 days’ notice. In personal-injury cases the plaintiff must file Form 30 interrogatory answers, special-damage documents, and, if lost wages are claimed, 3 years of tax returns with the complaint (waivable for good cause); a plaintiff in an action on a written instrument for payment of money or similar claims under 10 Del. C. § 3901 may note on the complaint that the defendant must answer by affidavit.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The plaintiff prepares the writ for the Prothonotary’s signature and seal; once it is on the electronic docket, the plaintiff delivers the service documents to the sheriff or a specially appointed process server (special appointment requires a non est return, service after 10:00 p.m. or on a weekend/holiday, or another exigent reason, except freely for Secretary of State service under 10 Del. C. §§ 3104, 3112, 3114). It covers summons service on individuals, minors, incompetents, entities, and governments; foreign/mesne attachment (affidavit, bond, publication within 20 days, defendant’s answer within 40 days of attachment); capias; scire facias and ejectment notices to lienholders and tenants within 10 days of filing; writs returnable 20 days after issuance; long-arm return receipts filed within 10 days of receipt; and dismissal without prejudice if the summons and complaint are not served within 120 days after filing absent good cause.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by delivery or mail, with mail service complete on mailing; garnishees answer within 20 days and the plaintiff may except within 10 days, with a $20 garnishee fee per garnishee; withdrawal of an appearance needs a court order unless Delaware counsel remains. Discovery requests and responses are served but not filed (a “Notice of Service” is filed instead), and in personal-injury cases the defendant serves Form 30 answers with the answer. Under Rule 5(g) as amended effective March 20, 2026, filed papers are public; a plaintiff may file a complaint under seal without an order if it gives notice by 3:00 p.m. the next day to interested persons and files public versions within 3 days, other documents need a good-cause sealing order with redacted public versions within 30 days, an application to keep a challenged record sealed is due within 7 days of an objection, and 90 days after an unappealed final judgment the Prothonotary notifies designators, who have 45 days to seek further confidential treatment.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or day the Prothonotary’s office is closed; for periods under 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded (legal holidays are those set by statute or by the Governor or Chief Justice). The Court may enlarge time for cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but not the time under Rules 50(b), 59(b), (d) and (e), or 60(b) except as those rules allow. Motions and notice of hearing must be served at least 2 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 1 day before; 3 days are added after service by mail, but only for acts by parties, not the Court.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"The pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer; the Court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, be filed without a backer, and be signed under Rule 11; demurrers and pleas are abolished, and papers go on white paper about 8½″ x 11″.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; defenses must be stated in short and plain terms with denials fairly meeting the averments, and listed affirmative defenses (including contributory negligence, injury by fellow servant, statute of limitations) must be pleaded. Averments not denied are admitted except as to damages; unlike the federal rule, Rule 8(dd) deems the existence of a corporation or partnership, signatures on a sued-on instrument, and a motor-vehicle operator’s agency admitted unless denied by affidavit served with the answer.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be alleged but must be challenged by specific negative averment; fraud, negligence and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (Delaware adds negligence to the federal list), while intent and knowledge may be averred generally. A pleading seeking unliquidated damages must not state an amount (except items of special damage), but on written request the pleader must serve a statement of the amount claimed within 10 days, which is not filed absent court order.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Pleadings need a caption with the court, title, file number and Rule 7(a) designation, numbered paragraphs, and separate counts where helpful; exhibits are part of the pleading. A complaint may not be filed under a pseudonym without prior court approval or an accompanying motion supported by an affidavit of specific facts showing why anonymity is necessary, which may be filed under seal.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers: Representations to Court, sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by at least 1 attorney of record (or the unrepresented party), certifying after reasonable inquiry that it has a proper purpose and legal and factual support. A sanctions motion must be served but not filed unless the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days; sanctions are limited to what deters repetition, no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal contentions, and the rule does not apply to discovery under Rules 26–37.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections — When and how presented — By pleading or motion — Motion for judgment on pleadings","s":"A defendant must serve an answer within 20 days after service of process and complaint (or 20 days after appearing before service), and answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are also due in 20 days; if a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, the responsive pleading is due 10 days after notice of the ruling (or 10 days after a more definite statement is served). Special timing applies to certiorari (exceptions within 10 days after the record is filed), ditch-return objections (10 days), mechanics’-lien exceptions (20 days), de novo appeals (20 days after service, or 40 days after the date of process if unserved), attachment (20 days after appearance), and capias (20 days after discharge on bail). The seven listed defenses (subject-matter and personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service, failure to state a claim, failure to join under Rule 19) may be raised by motion; a motion to strike against an unanswerable pleading is due within 20 days; personal-jurisdiction, venue, and process defenses are waived if omitted; Delaware’s 20-day answer period differs from the 21 days in FRCP 12(a).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, with exceptions for claims already pending elsewhere or where the opposing party sued by attachment; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed the opposing claim. Later-maturing and omitted counterclaims may be added with leave, cross-claims against coparties are allowed if transactionally related, and new parties may be joined under Rules 19 and 20.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on someone who may be liable to it for the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise on motion with notice. The third-party defendant may raise defenses and claims as provided, and a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may bring in a third party on the same terms.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise leave (freely given) or written consent is needed, and responses to amended pleadings are due within the time left or 10 days, whichever is longer. Rule 15(aa) requires the amended pleading to indicate plainly how it differs from the original; amendments may conform to the evidence, and relation back of a changed party requires notice within the period for service of the summons and complaint.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The Court may hold pretrial conferences and generally must enter a scheduling order setting deadlines for joinder, amendment, motions, discovery, and compulsory ADR (mediation by default if the parties cannot agree on a format); if the parties cannot agree on an ADR practitioner, they must jointly move within 30 days of the scheduling order for the Court to appoint one. ADR practitioners are immune absent bad faith, mediation communications are confidential and cannot be used later, and compulsory ADR does not apply to class actions, Rule 81(a) matters, replevin, attachment, statutory penalty, mortgage foreclosure, and in forma pauperis actions unless ordered. Changes to scheduling-order deadlines require an application or stipulated order, a party who fails to promptly report another’s missed deadline may waive objections, a final pretrial conference produces a pretrial stipulation (Form 46), and violations may draw Rule 37(b) sanctions and mandatory expenses.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Mandatory non-binding arbitration","s":"Civil actions seeking money damages (with only nominal nonmonetary claims) go to mandatory non-binding arbitration (MNA) when claimant’s counsel elects it on the Case Information Sheet, with the arbitrator’s authority capped at $50,000 exclusive of costs and interest; listed actions (class actions, Rule 81(a) matters, replevin, declaratory judgment, attachment, interpleader, summary proceedings, foreclosure, some in forma pauperis, statutory penalties) are excluded. Parties must select an arbitrator within 20 days after the close of initial pleadings or lose MNA, the hearing is held within 120 days of the close of pleadings on at least 10 days’ notice, exhibits are exchanged 10 days before, personal-injury claimants give HIPAA authorizations and records within 5 days, the arbitrator is paid for at least 3 hours, and the order is filed within 5 days after the hearing. Any party may demand a trial de novo within 20 days after the order is filed; a demanding party who does not do better at trial pays the arbitration costs and arbitrator’s fee, a party who skipped the hearing must pay all fees and costs before demanding trial de novo, and awards have no collateral estoppel effect.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"16.2","t":"[Deleted]","s":"Deleted; the text lists Rule 16.2 only as “[Deleted].”","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though fiduciaries and parties to contracts for another’s benefit may sue in their own names, and no dismissal occurs until a reasonable time is allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. An infant or incompetent person may sue or defend through a representative, next friend or guardian ad litem, whom the Court must appoint if needed; subdivision (b) is omitted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party asserting a claim may join as many claims, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party; subdivision (b) is omitted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person must be joined if feasible when complete relief is otherwise impossible or the person’s interest would be impaired or parties exposed to inconsistent obligations. If joinder is not feasible, the Court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed using four listed factors, and pleadings must name unjoined required persons and explain why.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise out of the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact. The Court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay or prejudice.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be joined and required to interplead even if their claims are not of common origin, and a defendant may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The remedy supplements the statutory remedy, but statutory actions proceed under these Rules.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality and adequacy, plus one of the (b)(1), (b)(2) or (b)(3) grounds; for (b)(3) classes the Court must give the best practicable notice, including the right to opt out by a specified date. The Court decides certification as soon as practicable and may manage the action by order; a class action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice to the class, except a dismissal without prejudice to the class where no compensation passed to the plaintiff or counsel.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention is of right when a statute confers an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and is permissive when a statute confers a conditional right or the applicant shares a common question of law or fact (state officers and agencies may intervene where a party relies on a statute or regulation they administer). The motion must be timely, served under Rule 5, and accompanied by a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On a party’s death, any party or the decedent’s successors or representatives may move to substitute; unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, the action is dismissed as to the decedent (except where an interlocutory appeal application has been made). The Rule also covers incompetency, transfers of interest, and automatic substitution of a successor when a public officer sued in an official capacity leaves office.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Parties may discover any non-privileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case, including ESI, and insurance agreements are discoverable; trial-preparation materials require substantial need and undue hardship, expert discovery proceeds mainly by interrogatory, and draft expert reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected. Effective September 30, 2026, new Rule 26(b)(3) defines a litigation-funding agreement (a non-attorney counterparty pays litigation expenses with outcome-contingent repayment and a right to control aspects of the litigation, such as approving settlement) and allows discovery only of its existence, parties, how it meets the definition, and what the funder may control—not the agreement itself, case information given to the funder, or the funder’s work product—though for cause the Court may order production of the control provisions, and the agreement is inadmissible at trial (former (b)(3)–(7) are renumbered (b)(4)–(8)). Protective orders are available for good cause, privilege withholding must be described, responses must be supplemented in listed situations, objections to a discovery-conference motion are due 10 days after service, and discovery papers must be signed with a certification under threat of sanctions.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"27","t":"Deposition before action or pending appeal","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text has no counterpart to this rule.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to give oaths where the examination occurs or a court-appointed person; abroad, under a treaty or convention, letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. A relative, employee, attorney or financially interested person may not serve as the officer.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Parties may by written stipulation set deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures, but stipulations extending the response time under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after the action begins, but a plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after serving any defendant unless the defendant has sought discovery or a special notice shows the witness is about to leave Delaware; notice must state the recording method, and organizations must designate witnesses under (b)(6); remote depositions are allowed by stipulation or order. From start to finish of a deposition, including recesses under five calendar days, the deponent’s attorney may not confer with the deponent about the substance of testimony (except about privilege or a court order) or suggest answers, and instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, court-ordered limits, or a motion to terminate. The witness has 30 days after notice of availability to review and sign; for depositions more than 150 miles from the courthouse the Court may require the noticing party to pay an adversary’s attorney’s attendance costs; the Rule also covers out-of-state evidence for the Industrial Accident Board and requires leave to depose Superior Court employees about official duties.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Any party may take a deposition on written questions served with a notice naming the deponent and officer. Cross questions are due within 14 days after service, redirect questions within 7 days after cross, and a further round within 7 days after redirect (the text of that last clause is garbled); the Court may enlarge or shorten these times.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, outside Delaware (unless procured by the offeror), unable to attend, not subpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition is not usable against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order that is still pending; objections to written-question form are waived unless served within the time for the next questions and within 5 days after the last questions, and nonstenographic depositions offered at trial require a transcript.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons; answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must be specific or are waived; attorney-signed answers to the required Form 30 interrogatories (Rules 3(h) and 5) must be followed by party-sworn answers within 30 days; contention interrogatories are allowed and business records may be specified in lieu of answering. The Delaware text sets no numerical limit on interrogatories.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things and entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","s":"A party may request inspection and copying of documents, ESI and tangible things, or entry on land, describing items with reasonable particularity. Responses are due within 30 days (a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint); objections must be specific and state whether materials are being withheld; production may be of copies by the inspection date or other stated reasonable time, and ESI is produced in the requested form or a reasonably usable form. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations of persons","s":"When a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group) is in controversy, the Court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner on motion for good cause, specifying time, place, manner, conditions and scope. The examined party may obtain the examiner’s detailed report but then must provide like reports and waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may request admission of facts, the application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents; each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry, the requester may move to test the sufficiency of answers, and admissions are conclusive for the pending action only unless the Court permits withdrawal.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery: Sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel answers, designations or production, and expenses generally follow the result; disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt, plus mandatory expenses. If ESI that should have been preserved is lost and cannot be replaced, the Court may order curative measures on a finding of prejudice, but adverse inferences, adverse-inference instructions, dismissal or default require a finding of recklessness or intent to deprive (a lower bar than the intent-only standard in FRCP 37(e)). Under Rule 37(e) as amended effective March 20, 2026, motions under Rules 26(c), 26(d) and 37 need a meet-and-confer certification (except Rule 37(d) motions), must be filed at least 10 days before presentation and may not exceed 6 pages (up from 4), oppositions of up to 6 pages are due at least 4 days before presentation, there is no reply, argument is limited to 15 minutes total, and a nonappearing attorney is assessed a fee of at least $100 and may file nothing further until it is paid.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The jury right is preserved as it has existed; a party must serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (if endorsed on a pleading, on the first page right after the caption), and other parties have 10 days to demand jury trial of additional issues. A party who does not demand a jury of 12 is deemed to consent to a jury of 6, and any other party has 10 days to demand 12; failure to demand waives the jury (or the 12-person jury), and a demand may not be withdrawn without consent.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the Court","s":"Cases with a 12-person demand are docketed “jury action (12)” and cases with a 6-person jury are docketed “jury action (6),” and are tried that way unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial or the Court finds no jury right; the Court may on motion order a 12-person jury despite no demand. Issues not demanded are tried by the Court, which may still order a jury on motion, use an advisory jury, or with consent try nonjury issues to a binding jury.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of judges; special juries; continuances","s":"The President Judge is the Assignment Judge and may, on motion for good cause, designate a judge for all proceedings in a case. A special jury must be requested at or before the case is marked for trial; a continuance motion for an absent material witness must be filed promptly with an affidavit of the expected testimony, efforts to secure the witness, and when the absence became known, and may be refused if the other side stipulates to the testimony.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss without order (except in replevin) by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all appearing parties, normally without prejudice (a second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits); otherwise dismissal requires a court order. Involuntary dismissals (other than for jurisdiction, venue or Rule 19) are on the merits unless stated otherwise; the Court may dismiss sua sponte after a show-cause notice requiring a response within 15 days, and a case with no proceedings for 6 months gets a notice and is dismissed if nothing happens within 30 days. When a party files bankruptcy, the case is stayed and moved to the dormant docket and is dismissed 24 months after transfer unless a party seeks an extension for good cause (12 months for cases pending when the rule took effect).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation: Separate trials","s":"The Court may consolidate or jointly try actions sharing a common question of law or fact and may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"42.1","t":"Opening statements by attorneys","s":"In jury trials the plaintiff’s attorney may open, and the defendant’s attorney may open either before any plaintiff testimony or after the plaintiff rests and before the defense case.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony; conferences during trial","s":"Testimony is taken in open court, though for good cause in compelling circumstances the Court may permit contemporaneous transmission from another location; sidebar and chambers conferences during trial are recorded unless the judge determines in advance that no evidentiary or substantive issues are involved. Affirmations may replace oaths, motions may be heard on affidavits, and the Court may appoint and fix compensation for interpreters.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official records","s":"Domestic official records may be proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custody certificate; foreign records by attested copy with a final certification (or, for good cause, without one), or as provided by treaty. A certified statement of diligent search proves the absence of a record, and other lawful methods remain available.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party raising foreign law must give notice in its pleadings or other reasonable written notice; the Court may consider any relevant source, admissible or not, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue from the county where the action is pending, are signed in blank by the Prothonotary or issued and signed by a Delaware attorney, must recite subdivisions (c)–(e), and may be served by the Sheriff or any nonparty at least 18 years old, with prior notice to parties of document production. A person commanded to produce may serve written objections within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner), after which production requires a court order; the Court must quash or modify subpoenas that allow unreasonable time, require privileged matter, or impose undue burden, and disobedience may be contempt.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and its grounds when the ruling is sought or made, and a party with no chance to object is not prejudiced.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The Court conducts voir dire, and attorneys’ proposed questions must be submitted in writing before jury drawing begins. Each party gets 3 peremptory challenges (multiple plaintiffs or defendants may be treated as one party, and the Court may allow more for good cause); up to 6 alternates may be seated, with 1, 2 or 3 extra peremptories for 1–2, 3–4 or 5–6 alternates, usable only against alternates.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than 12 — Majority verdict","s":"The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to a verdict by a stated majority.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"The Court may require a special verdict on each fact issue, and an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires. The Court may also submit interrogatories with a general verdict and, if the answers and verdict conflict, enter judgment on consistent answers, return the jury, or order a new trial.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law in actions tried by jury; alternative motion for new trial; conditional rulings","s":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made before the case goes to the jury, specifying the judgment sought and its legal and factual basis. A denied motion may be renewed by service and filing no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, with or alternatively to a Rule 59 new-trial motion, and the losing party on a granted motion may move for a new trial within 10 days after entry of judgment; the Court must make conditional new-trial rulings (Delaware keeps 10 days where FRCP 50 now allows 28).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury; objection","s":"Parties may file written requested instructions at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the Court must tell counsel its rulings before closing arguments. An instruction error is preserved only by an objection stating the matter and grounds, made before or at the time the Court sets immediately after the jury retires, outside the jury’s hearing.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the Court","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text contains no Rule 52 (Rule 6(b) also lists Rule 52(b) as omitted).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Omitted from this part of the Rules; masters in Superior Court are addressed in Rules 113–123.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs","s":"A judgment includes any appealable order; in multi-claim or multi-party cases, partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Costs are allowed to the prevailing party on application within 10 days of entry of final judgment; court-reporter, video, and deposition witness and expert fees are taxable only if the deposition is introduced in evidence, unnecessary costs may be taxed against the party causing them, and attorney appearance fees are never taxed as costs.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"54.1","t":"Fees for the prothonotary","s":"Repealed, effective July 1, 1988.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"55","t":"Default judgments","s":"When a party fails to appear, plead or defend, the Prothonotary enters default judgment on a sum-certain claim on written direction with a statement of principal (not exceeding the complaint’s demand), credits and interest and an affidavit that the defendant is not an infant or incompetent, is in default, and owes the amount. Other defaults require application to the Court, with at least 3 days’ written notice to a party who has appeared and hearings or a jury as needed; special provisions cover capias, Rule 3(c) appeals, and confession judgments (Rules 58.1–58.3), and defaults are set aside under Rule 60(b).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move for summary judgment after 20 days from commencement (or after the opponent moves) and before the case is marked for trial, and a defending party may move at any time, though the Court may allow later motions after a pretrial conference. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, judgment is granted if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law, affidavits must be on personal knowledge, and the opponent must set out specific facts rather than rest on the pleadings (or show by affidavit why facts are unavailable). Bad-faith affidavits draw expenses and possible contempt, and cross-motions without argument that a material fact is disputed are deemed a stipulation for decision on the record.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions under Delaware’s statute follow these Rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the Court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case on the calendar.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Subject to Rule 54(b), the Prothonotary enters judgment at once on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief, while other relief, special verdicts and verdicts with interrogatories need the Court to approve the form first. A judgment is effective only when set forth and entered, entry is not delayed for costs, and attorneys may submit forms of judgment only when the Court directs.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58A","t":"Entry of judgment by confession and execution thereon","s":"Transferred; see Rule 58.1.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58B","t":"Entry of judgment by confession in open court","s":"Transferred; see Rule 58.2.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58C","t":"Judgments by confession entered prior to July 9, 1971","s":"Transferred; see Rule 58.3.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58.1","t":"Entry of judgment by confession and execution thereon","s":"To confess judgment under 10 Del. C. § 2306, the plaintiff lodges a praecipe in the prescribed form, the original instrument with copies, any nonresident affidavit, and § 2306(b) notice letters with certified-mail envelopes; the Prothonotary dockets the judgment tentatively and mails each debtor notice that it may object on the second motion day after mailing (or by publication once a week for 2 weeks if mail is undelivered). Judgment is entered if the debtor does not appear; if the debtor appears, the plaintiff must prove an effective waiver of notice and hearing, and the lien relates back to the original docketing. Before the first writ of execution, a similar § 2306(j) notice-and-hearing procedure applies.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58.2","t":"Entry of judgment by confession in open court","s":"An obligee may obtain a confessed judgment in open court on a motion day, appearing with the obligor before a court reporter, by filing the Rule 58.1 praecipe and the original instrument and proving the obligation’s genuineness, the obligor’s identity and signature, and an effective waiver of constitutional rights. The judgment is final like one after trial, and execution follows Rule 58.1(h).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"58.3","t":"Judgments by confession entered prior to July 9, 1971","s":"Before the first execution on a confessed judgment entered before July 9, 1971, the creditor files a praecipe and § 2306(f) notice letters, which the Prothonotary mails (or publishes once a week for 2 weeks); the debtor may object on the second motion day after mailing. If the debtor contests, the creditor must file a complaint within 10 days alleging an effective waiver and the debt, and the judgment is stricken, sustained, or limited depending on who prevails on waiver and indebtedness (or stricken if the creditor fails to appear or file).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials and rearguments","s":"A new-trial motion must be served and filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (or after the verdict if entry was deferred) with a brief and any affidavits; the opponent has 10 days to answer, and with affidavits reply papers are due 10 days later, each extendable once by up to 10 days by the Court or stipulation. The Court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days after entry of judgment, and a motion to alter or amend a judgment is due within 10 days after entry. A motion for reargument must be served and filed within 5 days after the Court’s opinion or decision is filed, with any answer due 5 days after service (Delaware’s 10-day new-trial period is shorter than the 28 days under FRCP 59, and the reargument motion has no direct FRCP counterpart).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time. On motion and on just terms the Court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void or satisfied judgment, or any other justifying reason; the motion does not suspend the judgment, independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available, and old writs such as coram nobis and audita querela are abolished. The text of this consolidation states no time limit for the motion.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors in evidence rulings or other acts are not grounds for a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice, and the Court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"62","t":"Stays by trial court and on appeal","s":"No execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after entry of judgment (FRCP 62 now allows 30 days), and the Court may stay enforcement pending Rule 50, 59, or 60 motions on security. On appeals from a lower court to Superior Court, a stay may require a supersedeas bond or cash deposit covering the judgment plus interest and costs; stays pending appeal to the Supreme Court are governed by Article IV, § 24 of the Delaware Constitution and Supreme Court Rule 32, and statutory stays and Rule 54(b) stays are also available.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"If a judge cannot continue a trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice; in a bench trial the successor must, on request, recall any available material disputed witness.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of persons or property","s":"Statutory seizure remedies to compel appearance or secure satisfaction of a judgment are available during the action. On a defendant’s motion for discharge on common bail the Court sets a hearing, and the defendant is discharged unless the plaintiff files an affidavit to hold to special bail; reasonable notice of justification is required when the Prothonotary takes special bail.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"64.1","t":"Orders for hearing or rules to show cause","s":"Except where a statute requires a rule to show cause, matters formerly brought that way are started by motion after a complaint or petition is filed, may be presented ex parte as to nonappearing persons, and the order sets the hearing and notice deadlines and is served like a summons (publication allowed on a showing). A rule to show cause, used only where a statute requires it, must state the return date and whether a hearing will be held.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 65.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security: Proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety on a bond or undertaking submits to the Court’s jurisdiction and appoints the Prothonotary as agent for service, and its liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the Prothonotary mailing copies to known sureties.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers appointed by federal courts","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 66.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in Court","s":"In actions for money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of Court, deposit all or part of it with the Court and must serve the order on the Prothonotary. Money is withdrawn only by court order and is held in an interest-bearing account or court-approved instrument.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days to accept in writing, after which judgment is entered. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, and an offeree who does not obtain a more favorable judgment must pay costs incurred after the offer; after liability is determined, an offer may be made at least 10 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Execution may issue at any time while the judgment remains a lien on the debtor’s real property, the fictitious writ of vice comes is abolished, and the creditor may take discovery in aid of execution; execution on confessed judgments follows Rules 58.1–58.3. Security for a stay of execution after certain affidavit-of-defense defaults must be given within 20 days after judgment, payable in six months; sheriff’s sale returns are made on the third Monday of the following month, objections are due by the next Thursday, and unobjected sales are confirmed that Friday. Rules for writs of possession are served like a summons or posted at least 6 days before the return day, and no sheriff’s sale of real estate may proceed unless at least 7 days before the sale the plaintiff sends certified-mail notice (Form 37) to lienholders, tenants, terre tenants and record interest holders of at least 30 days’ standing, posts it, and files proof.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 70.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"Omitted; the Delaware text has no Rule 71.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"71A","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"Transferred; see Rule 71.1.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"In condemnation by a public agency (state, county, municipality or school district), an order of possession under 10 Del. C. § 6110(a) is entered forthwith on 10 days’ written notice and an affidavit of necessity from the agency’s chief administrative officer, unless the owner shows good cause by affidavits, depositions or verified answer, with any hearing held without delay. The owner bears the burden of overcoming the presumption of regularity and the prima facie case of public necessity, and the Court may still enter possession ex parte under the statute.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"71.2","t":"Medical negligence","s":"Repealed, effective August 13, 2025 (by the order effective September 1, 2025).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"71.3","t":"Forfeitures pursuant to 16 Del. C. § 4784","s":"The State must give notice of a drug-related seizure within 60 days (also by first-class mail to any incarcerated interest holder), stating that claimants may petition for return within 45 days after the notice. After 45 days from the last notice, the State may apply under oath for forfeiture in rem; a timely sworn petition for return must be served on the Attorney General and is tried without a jury within 90 days unless a jury is demanded, with the claimant bearing the burden by a preponderance of the evidence. Lienholders with knowledge of the unlawful use also forfeit, a conviction precludes denying the offense, and an acquittal or dismissal does not bar forfeiture (amended effective September 1, 2025).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"72","t":"Appeals from certain commissioners, boards and courts","s":"For appeals on the record from boards, commissions, personnel hearing officers and courts, the notice of appeal is filed within the statutory time or, if none, 15 days after the final order, and copies are mailed to all parties with a certificate; an Industrial Accident Board claimant accepting part of an award must say so. The Prothonotary issues a citation requiring the agency to send the certified record within 20 days (the transcript may be omitted by stipulation filed within 10 days); the appellant’s opening brief is due 20 days after the record is filed, the answering brief 20 days later, and any reply 10 days after that. Cross-appeals are due within 10 days after the first notice of appeal, nonresident appellants may be required to post security for costs, and sua sponte dismissal requires a show-cause notice with a response due within 10 days of receipt.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"72.1","t":"Expedited procedure for appeals on the record","s":"Deleted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"73","t":"Preparation of transcript on appeal to the Supreme Court","s":"On appeals under Supreme Court Rule 7, counsel must follow Supreme Court Rule 9(e)(ii)–(iv) on designating the record, court reporters must follow Rule 9(e) on transcripts, and unrecorded or stipulated proceedings are handled under Supreme Court Rule 9(g).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"74","t":"Interlocutory appeals to the Supreme Court","s":"Interlocutory appeals from Superior Court go forward on the terms and procedures of Supreme Court Rule 42.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"75","t":"Certification of questions of law to the Supreme Court","s":"Certification of questions of law follows Supreme Court Rule 41; a petition asking Superior Court to certify must set out the facts and issues clearly and attach a proposed certification substantially in the form of Supreme Court Official Form K. If certification is ordered, the Prothonotary files 6 certified copies with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 5 days.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"76","t":"Supreme Court mandate","s":"When the Supreme Court reverses or modifies a judgment or further proceedings are needed, prevailing counsel prepares and submits an appropriate order, timed on remand so the Superior Court can comply with Supreme Court Rule 19(c).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"77","t":"Superior Courts; Prothonotaries, records and exhibits, fees","s":"The Court is always open for business, merits trials are in open court, the Prothonotary mails notice of every order or judgment (lack of notice does not extend appeal time), notifies the Bar of rule amendments within 10 days, and keeps dockets (electronically if desired); exhibits must be removed after the appeal period and may be disposed of if not removed within 15 days of notice, and court reporters keep notes for 10 years. Written opinions bear the final-submission date and the filing date. The fee schedule includes a $200 filing fee for most complaints and appeals ($250 for the Complex Commercial Litigation Division; 0.005 times the amount in controversy, minimum $200 and maximum $5,000, for Summary Proceedings for Commercial Disputes), $245 per additional 50 filings, $150 to request a trial date or pretrial conference, $65 per writ of execution, a $10 Court Security Assessment on initial filings with fees of $200 or more, a $25 fine for fees unpaid 10 days after written demand, and Commissioner mediation at a minimum of 2 hours at $150 per hour, paid within 20 days of notice of appointment.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion days; arguments","s":"Unless the Court orders otherwise, New Castle County motions not before an assigned judge are heard Fridays at 1:30 p.m., Kent County motions Fridays at 11 a.m. (Commissioner motions Thursdays at 2:00 p.m.), and Sussex County motions on the 1st and 3rd Fridays at 11:00 a.m. Motions and responses may not exceed 6 pages, responses are due no later than 4 days before the hearing, and papers must be double-spaced in Times New Roman 14-point type (footnotes single-spaced in 12-point) with two spaces between sentences and italicized or underlined case names. There is no oral argument unless the Court schedules it.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the Prothonotary","s":"The Prothonotary keeps a docket of all civil actions with consecutive file numbers and chronological entries for every filing, process, order and judgment. Entries for judicial action must show the date and judge for arguments, trials (with elapsed trial time), verdicts and opinions (noting if decided without argument), plus other records the judges or the court administrator require.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"79.1","t":"Electronic filing","s":"The President Judge designates which civil cases or categories are eFile cases and sets the administrative procedures, and a technology surcharge of $1.25 per document is assessed in each eFile case to run the system. A Delaware lawyer may authorize only firm employees or a retained service provider to eFile for the lawyer, no one may use another’s password, an eFiling by or under a lawyer’s authorization counts as that lawyer’s Rule 11 signature, every eFiling must be signed by a Delaware Bar member or a self-represented party, and electronic service under the procedures counts as Rule 5 service unless ordered otherwise.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"80","t":"Seal","s":"The Court’s seal reads “SUPERIOR COURT” on the upper arc and “STATE OF DELAWARE” on the lower arc around arms like those on the Great Seal of the State, and the Prothonotary may affix it to appropriate documents.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability in special proceedings","s":"In listed special matters—boundaries, change of name, condemnation, contested elections, ditches, dower, drainage, ejectment, escheat, ground rent, habeas corpus, insolvency, mandamus, mills, prohibition, quo warranto, satisfaction of mortgages and judgments, waste, and wrecks—procedure follows these Rules so far as practicable and consistent with statute, and otherwise prior practice; subdivisions (b) and (c) are repealed.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The Rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction or affect venue.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"83","t":"Rules by district courts","s":"Omitted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"Omitted (court forms are posted at courts.delaware.gov/forms, per the Appendix of Forms note).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"The rules may be cited as the Superior Court Rules of Civil Procedure.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"The Rules took effect January 1, 1948, and the 1965 and 1991 amendment packages took effect January 1, 1965 and January 1, 1991; each applies to new actions and to further proceedings in pending actions unless the Court finds application infeasible or unjust.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"87","t":"Assignment of causes to Family Court","s":"Transfers of causes to the Family Court are made by a Superior Court judge’s certificate naming the parties, the nature of the matter, and the issues to be decided, and directing the Prothonotary to deliver the certificate and designated pleadings and exhibits to the Family Court clerk; subdivisions (b) and (c) are repealed.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"87.1","t":"Assignment of cases to the Court of Common Pleas","s":"Transfers to the Court of Common Pleas under 10 Del. C. ch. 13 are made by a certificate signed by a Superior Court judge that includes the caption and directs the Prothonotary to deliver it with designated pleadings and exhibits.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"88","t":"Allowance of attorney’s fee","s":"Before awarding fees to an appointed attorney or an attorney for a party without funds, the Court requires an affidavit or letter stating any amounts received or to be received from other sources.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"89","t":"Appointment of counsel for State officers and employees","s":"Appointment of counsel for State officials and employees under 60 Del. Laws c. 474 is governed by Supreme Court Rule 68.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"90","t":"Attorneys","s":"Except under Rule 90.1, only members of the Delaware Supreme Court Bar who maintain a Delaware office for the practice of law may practice in Superior Court. No attorney may withdraw without court order, withdrawal is ordinarily not grounds for delay, and agreements between attorneys are enforced only if written and filed or stated on the record.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"90.1","t":"Admission pro hac vice","s":"Out-of-state attorneys may be admitted in the Court’s discretion only on written motion by a Delaware Bar member with a Delaware office (“Delaware Counsel”), filed separately in each court and no later than the applicant’s first appearance, with Delaware Counsel certifying that the applicant is reputable and competent. The applicant certifies good standing elsewhere, being bound by the Delaware Lawyers’ Rules of Professional Conduct (and having reviewed the Statement of Principles of Lawyer Conduct), that the attorney and firm colleagues on the matter are bound by the Court’s rules, consent to the Prothonotary as agent for service, every Delaware case appeared in during the preceding 12 months, any discipline or pending proceedings, and all admissions; the attorney pays the Supreme Court’s assessment ($375 in 2015, $400 in 2016, then indexed annually to inflation), renewable each January 1 and delinquent if unpaid by February 1, with no proration. Delaware Counsel must appear, sign or receive service of all papers, and attend all proceedings unless excused (depositions only if ordered); the Court weighs whether the applicant is effectively practicing in Delaware without admission, may revoke admission after an opportunity to respond, and withdrawal follows Rule 90(b).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"91-99","t":"[Omitted]","s":"Rules 91 through 99 are listed as omitted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"100","t":"Divorce and annulment","s":"Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"101","t":"Process","s":"Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"102","t":"Sheriffs to whom writs shall be issued","s":"Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"103","t":"Mailing of petition","s":"Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"104","t":"Answer in contested divorce","s":"Repealed, effective October 23, 1972.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"104A","t":"Regular trial days","s":"Transferred.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"104.1","t":"Regular trial days","s":"Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"105","t":"Necessity of payment of costs","s":"Repealed, effective October 15, 1980.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"106","t":"Removal of causes from Court of Common Pleas","s":"A case removed from the Court of Common Pleas proceeds without further process as though a complaint had been served on the date the original process was served.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"107","t":"Briefs","s":"File the original and a copy of every brief with the Prothonotary (extra copies if more than one judge sits), double-spaced in Times New Roman 14-point with 14-point single-spaced footnotes and two spaces between sentences, on roughly 8½″ x 11″ paper bound on the left, with a cover, tables of contents and citations, a statement of the case (first brief), questions involved, and argument; citations follow the Bluebook without parallel cites, and unreported opinions not on Westlaw or Lexis must be attached. Without leave, opening and answering briefs may not exceed 8,000 words and reply briefs 5,500 words, exclusive of the tables and appendix, with a certificate of compliance stating the word count (Form 48); parties without word processing are limited to 40 pages and 25 pages. Briefing schedules are set by the Court and extensions require an order on good cause even with consent; late papers can lead to dismissal, abandonment, or summary disposition, expenses are awarded on motions to compel compliance, and an appendix may be submitted without duplicating the opponent’s.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"108","t":"Sureties","s":"Each surety company must file, every January, a power of attorney in each county where it does business before the Court will accept it as surety. No attorney or court officer, nor their agents, employees, immediate family, or controlled corporations, may serve as special bail or surety in a case in the Court, except where they are the principal on the bond.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"109","t":"Board of Canvass proceedings","s":"A complaint asking the Board of Canvass to act under 15 Del. C. § 5702 must be sworn and filed by 12:00 noon on the day the Board convenes (unless the Board extends the time) and state the candidate, office, districts, the fraud or mistake with particularity, the number of votes affected and whether it changes the result, and the basis of knowledge. It may be withdrawn only with the Board’s permission.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"110","t":"Condemnation cases; designation of proposed commissioners and formation of calendar","s":"Unless ordered otherwise, within 30 days after filing a condemnation complaint the plaintiff submits a blank order for designating proposed commissioners under 10 Del. C. § 6108, and striking must occur no later than 20 days after the order. Once commissioners are struck, the case goes on the condemnation trial calendar for the next term.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"111","t":"Proceeding for an order for the destruction of material in connection with a public health emergency","s":"Petitions to destroy material or property under 16 Del. C. § 508(c) or 20 Del. C. § 3134 are filed in the county where it is located, after scheduling a hearing that allows at least 5 days’ notice. The verified petition must describe the petitioner’s authority, the property, the public-health need, and all owners and interested persons, with an affidavit of notice and a proposed order; the Court may grant final or interim relief.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"112","t":"Proceedings in forma pauperis","s":"A party wishing to proceed without prepaying fees must apply with an affidavit, and the Court may waive fees or set a payment schedule, with accrued costs recovered from any later judgment or settlement. If the application is denied, the notice must state the filing fee and a payment date not less than 15 days from the notice to avoid dismissal.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"113","t":"Appointment; removal","s":"The Court may appoint a Master pro hac vice in any pending case; the appointment is effective when the Court signs the order, and the Master may be removed at the Court’s pleasure.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"114","t":"Duties and powers","s":"The Master regulates hearings under the order of reference and may administer oaths, examine parties and witnesses, compel attendance and documents, have evidence taken down or taken by commission or deposition, grant adjournments and extensions, and take other necessary steps, all subject to the Court’s revision and control.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"115","t":"Witnesses; documents","s":"Witnesses, including parties, are summoned before the Master by Prothonotary-issued subpoena to testify or produce documents; disobedience is contempt certified by the Master and enforced by attachment, and witnesses receive the same compensation as for court attendance.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"116","t":"Testimony taken and certified","s":"Oral testimony before a Master is taken by a stenographer the Master selects and swears, the Master certifies that the testimony was taken in the Master’s presence and is accurate, and the Master fixes the stenographer’s pay subject to court approval.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"117","t":"Objections to testimony; hearing thereon","s":"The Master rules on competency and admissibility, witnesses must answer as if directed by the Court, irrelevant questions are not answered, and exceptions are unnecessary but grounds must be stated specifically. After proofs close and before the report, a party may bring overruled objections (or exclusions of evidence) to the Court, which may direct further testimony or disregard of testimony.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"118","t":"Persons who may be examined; burden of proof on exceptions to claim","s":"The Master may examine any party, creditor or claimant orally or by written interrogatories and compel attendance by subpoena. When exceptions are taken to claims against a fund, the claimant bears the burden of proving the claim as filed and may not prove unlisted items without the Master’s order for good cause.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"119","t":"Time for taking testimony","s":"If the order of reference fixes dates for starting testimony and closing proofs, the Master cannot extend them; otherwise the Master sets a time and place promptly on reasonable notice and proceeds diligently, and any party may ask the Court to order the Master to speed the proceedings and explain delays.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"120","t":"Limiting time for taking testimony","s":"The Master may set a deadline for each party to close its proofs, extendable for good cause, and then report on the evidence submitted without waiting for a party who misses the deadline.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"121","t":"Minutes of proceedings to be kept","s":"The Master keeps minutes of all proceedings, noting attorney appearances, adjournments, hearing times and other matters showing how the reference was executed.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"122","t":"Master’s report; action by Court","s":"The Master circulates a draft report, hears written exceptions within the time the Master sets, and files a final report; exceptions to the final report are due within 10 days after notice of its filing is mailed and are limited to disallowed draft exceptions and changes from the draft. Any party may request a hearing, and the Court reviews the report de novo, on the record in whole or in part.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"123","t":"Inspection of documents","s":"When documents are ordered or subpoenaed to be produced before a Master, the Master decides which must be produced, when and how long they stay in the Master’s office, or directs inspection by the parties at a time, place and manner the Master sets.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"124","t":"Scope of rules","s":"Rules 124–131 govern Summary Proceedings for Commercial Disputes, available by written consent in matters within Superior Court jurisdiction (excluding personal, physical or mental injury claims) where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000 as to at least one party, exclusive of interest and costs, and at least one party is a Delaware citizen or entity. No punitive damages or jury trial are available; the Court may, on written motion to the President Judge or designee, accept smaller cases if resources allow, and the other civil rules apply where consistent.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"125","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A Summary Proceeding starts by filing and serving a complaint, sent by next-day delivery to the agreed notice person or each defendant’s principal place of business or residence (plaintiff supplies prepaid envelopes and files proof of receipt), stating prominently on page one that Summary Proceedings are requested, the amount in controversy, the Delaware party, and the defendant’s consent, with the consent document attached. Eligible actions may be transferred or converted by consent, with a scheduling conference within 15 days, and nothing is docketed until the filing fee is paid.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"125.1","t":"Summary proceedings for Commercial Disputes Panel","s":"The President Judge assigns Summary Proceedings to a judge on the Summary Proceedings for Commercial Disputes Panel, appointed by the President Judge from the Superior Court judges, and the Prothonotary keeps a public list of Panel judges.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"126","t":"Responses to the complaint","s":"The answer and any compulsory counterclaims are due within 30 days after service, replies to counterclaims within 20 days (each with a list of persons consulted), and cross-claims, permissive counterclaims and third-party claims require all parties’ agreement. A motion to dismiss with brief is due within 30 days (20 days for counterclaims), the answering brief within 15 days, and the reply within 10 days, with opening and answering briefs limited to 25 pages and replies to 10 pages; the Court rules or gives an estimate within 30 days after final briefing or argument (normally no more than 30 more days), and an answer is due 10 days after a denial.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"127","t":"Depositions and discovery","s":"The plaintiff must serve trial documents, witness lists and persons consulted within 7 days after the answer, and the defendant must reciprocate within 30 days. Each party may serve up to 10 interrogatories and 10 requests for admission (subparts count separately) and document requests within 30 days after the last answer, with interrogatory and admission responses due in 20 days and document responses with production in 30 days; each party may notice up to 4 depositions (plus listed witnesses and Rule 129 affiants) on at least 7 days’ notice, the first notice within 60 days and all depositions done within 120 days after the last answer. Fact discovery closes 180 days after the last answer and expert discovery 60 days after that, and discovery disputes may be sent to a Master at the parties’ expense.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"128","t":"Summary judgment","s":"No summary judgment motions are allowed in Summary Proceedings.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"129","t":"Optional briefing procedure","s":"If the parties notify the Court within 7 days after discovery closes that they will forgo trial witnesses, the plaintiff’s brief is due 30 days after the close of discovery, the answering brief 30 days after service, and the reply 15 days after that, citing the record. Affiants must be identified before discovery closes and produced for deposition on request within two weeks after the date discovery would otherwise close, and the Court finds facts on the submitted record.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"130","t":"Trial","s":"If witnesses are waived, trial is oral argument (or submission on briefs) within one week after briefing closes; otherwise trial begins 30 to 60 days after discovery closes, an agreed pretrial order is due within 30 days after discovery closes, trial is limited to 5 days, and post-trial briefs of up to 50 pages with proposed findings are due within 10 days after trial. The Court decides or gives an estimate within 30 days after final briefing or argument (normally no more than 30 more days), and schedules may be extended only on the judge’s certification of complexity or competing criminal caseload.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"131","t":"Application to Court for modification of Rules","s":"The Summary Proceedings rules, except Rules 129 and 130, may be modified by agreement of all parties with court approval.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"132","t":"Commissioners","s":"Commissioners may administer oaths, serve as special masters, decide non-dispositive matters, and hear dispositive matters (including Title 16 ch. 50 mental hearings, judgment on the pleadings, summary judgment, and dismissal motions) and propose findings and recommendations to a judge. Objections are due within 10 days after the Commissioner’s order or proposed findings are filed, with responses due 10 days after service, and the objecting party must file a transcript (or agreed statement of facts); non-dispositive orders are reviewed only for clearly erroneous findings, legal error, or abuse of discretion and are not stayed absent order, while dispositive recommendations get de novo review. Judges may shorten or enlarge these times for good cause.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"133","t":"Single-transaction guardianship; settlement of tort claims for disabled person","s":"In settling a tort claim for a person with a disability (including a minor), the Court may approve the settlement and disbursements and, for minors, place net funds of a gross settlement of $25,000 or less in a UTMA account, or for larger settlements in a court-approved annuity or structured instrument (with up to $25,000 in a UTMA account), without appointing a property guardian; a guardian is appointed only on good cause where all funds are so placed, and guardianships transfer to the Court of Chancery. Annuities may not pay out before majority and proof must be filed within 60 days; petitions require an affidavit (Form 133B), a UTMA order consistent with Form 133A, medical or other evidence showing the settlement is fair, and the non-petitioning parent’s consent or reasons it cannot be obtained. Petitions under $50,000 gross are decided on the papers, others in open court with the person present, and all Rule 133 filings are deemed sealed without a motion (as amended effective September 1, 2025).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"134","t":"Scope of Rules for Mediation","s":"Rules 134–136 govern court mediation of business disputes under 10 Del. C. § 546, eligible for purely monetary claims only where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000; the mediator is a sitting judge or commissioner, and parties may change the rules with the mediator’s consent. The rule supplies model consent language for mediation in Superior Court under § 546.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"135","t":"Commencement of Mediation","s":"Mediation starts with a petition signed by Delaware counsel (as defined in Rule 90.1(a)) and the filing fee, which the Prothonotary sends by next-day delivery; it must state the issues, all parties’ consent, subject-matter jurisdiction, the business-entity and Delaware-connection requirements, that no party is a consumer, and the amount in controversy for monetary claims. The petition is confidential and not publicly docketed, the mediator sets the conference within 15 days of receipt and it generally occurs within 60 days, and there is no formal discovery.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"136","t":"Mediation Conference","s":"A representative with settlement authority and Delaware counsel for each party must attend; conferences are private, mediation communications and files are confidential (with listed exceptions), and mediators cannot be compelled to testify and are immune absent bad faith or willful misconduct. Any agreement is written and signed by the parties and mediator; if no agreement is reached the mediator terminates the mediation without prejudice and cannot later adjudicate the dispute, and fees follow the Prothonotary’s schedule.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"137","t":"Arbitration","s":"Rules 137–139 govern voluntary, binding court arbitration of business disputes under 10 Del. C. § 546, eligible for purely monetary claims only where the amount in controversy exceeds $100,000, before a sitting judge or commissioner who (absent agreement) did not mediate the dispute. Superior Court Rules 26–37 apply unless modified, and the rule defines the preliminary conference, preliminary hearing, scheduling order, arbitration hearing, and model consent-to-arbitrate language.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"138","t":"Commencement of Arbitration","s":"Arbitration starts with a petition signed by Delaware counsel and the filing fee, sent by the Prothonotary by next-business-day delivery, stating the dispute, parties, claims, remedy, consent, jurisdiction, the business-entity and Delaware-connection requirements, that no party is a consumer, and the amount for monetary claims. The petition is confidential unless appealed; the preliminary conference occurs within 10 days after commencement, a scheduling order follows the preliminary hearing, the hearing generally occurs within 90 days after receipt of the petition, and pre-hearing information exchange is agreed or directed by the arbitrator.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"139","t":"Arbitration Hearing","s":"A representative with authority and Delaware counsel for each party must attend; hearings are private, communications and files confidential (with listed exceptions), and arbitrators immune absent bad faith or willful misconduct. Parties may switch to court mediation or seek the arbitrator’s settlement help at any stage; the arbitrator may grant any just and equitable relief within the parties’ agreement, including interim awards, a final award is entered as a judgment, the arbitrator may not adjudicate later litigation on the issues, and filing and per-day fees follow the Prothonotary’s schedule.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"140","t":"Historical Society of the Superior Court of Delaware","s":"Establishes the Historical Society of the Superior Court of Delaware to preserve and educate about the Court’s history, its members and its administration of justice, made up of the sitting judges and other bench and bar members designated by the President Judge with the concurrence of a majority of the judges.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"141","t":"Scope of Complex Commercial Division Rules","s":"Effective March 20, 2026, Rules 141–146 govern cases in the Complex Commercial Litigation Division (CCLD), which takes cases with a claim of $1 million or more, cases involving an exclusive choice-of-court agreement or a judgment based on one, or cases designated by the President Judge; personal-injury, mortgage foreclosure, mechanics’ lien, and condemnation cases, and choice-of-court cases involving consumers or employment contracts, are excluded. A party designates a case by entering CCLD as the Civil Case Code and Complex Commercial Litigation as the Civil Case Type on the Case Information Statement, an opposing party moves before the Rule 16 scheduling conference (without affecting other deadlines), and the regular Civil Rules apply unless the CCLD Rules provide otherwise.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"142","t":"Public Access to Documents Filed with the Court in CCLD Actions","s":"Replacing Rule 5(g) in CCLD cases, filed documents are public unless they contain Confidential Information (confidentially maintained, not public, would cause particularized harm outweighing the public interest, such as trade secrets, medical records, Social Security and account numbers, birth dates and minors’ names), which may be filed as a Confidential Filing with a prescribed cover page and footer. The filer must give notice to interested persons—for an original complaint by 3:00 p.m. the next day, with redaction designations due by 3:00 p.m. on the third day after filing; otherwise by 3:00 p.m. two days after filing, with designations by 3:00 p.m. on the fifth day—and file a public version the day after the designation deadline (exhibits and lodged depositions excepted). Challenges may be filed no sooner than 10 days after the filing, motions to maintain confidentiality are due within 5 days of a challenge and oppositions within 5 days after that (the proponent bears the burden), confidential treatment expires 3 years after final disposition with 90 days’ advance notice and a 45-day window to move to extend, and the 3 extra mailing days of Rule 6(e) do not apply.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"143","t":"Amended and Supplemental Pleadings in CCLD","s":"In CCLD cases a party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is required and the case is not set for trial; otherwise written consent or leave (freely given) is needed, the amended pleading must be filed with a document showing how it differs, and amendments do not affect others’ counterclaims, crossclaims or third-party claims. A plaintiff facing a Rule 12(b)(6) motion must amend or seek leave before its response is due (or within 30 days after transfer from another court), or the dismissal is with prejudice as to that party unless the Court finds good cause. Responses to amended pleadings are due within the time remaining or 10 days, whichever is later, relation back for a changed party requires notice within 120 days of the complaint’s filing (or longer for good cause), and responses to supplemental pleadings are due within 10 days.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"144","t":"Additional Procedures for Filing Discovery Motions in CCLD","s":"In CCLD cases, motions under Rules 26(c), 26(d) and 37 (except those during an oral deposition) must include counsel’s certification of a reasonable effort to agree, detailing dates, time spent and method, which must include oral communication between Delaware counsel for the moving and opposing parties; Rule 37(d) motions are exempt. Failure to certify may result in dismissal of the motion unless the Court orders otherwise.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"145","t":"CCLD Motions","s":"CCLD requests for orders are made by written motion (unless at a hearing or trial) with numbered paragraphs and, preferably, a proposed order. A motion supported by an opening brief is limited to 500 words, and briefs are permitted only with motions under Rules 12, 41 and 56 (briefed under Rule 146); other motions and oppositions are limited to 3,000 words and replies to 2,000 words, excluding caption, title, signature block and sealing markings, and absent agreement or order oppositions are due within 7 days and replies within 5 days.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"146","t":"CCLD Briefs","s":"Rule 107 governs the form of CCLD briefs. Unless the parties agree in writing or the Court orders otherwise, the responsive brief is due within 30 days after the opening brief and the reply within 15 days after the response, and any party may request a hearing after the opening brief is filed, which the Court may grant or decide without.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 1","t":"Court of Chancery: Scope and Purpose","s":"The rules govern Chancery procedure and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every proceeding.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 3","t":"Court of Chancery: Commencing an Action; Deposits, Fees","s":"An action starts with a complaint plus a supplemental information sheet; listed pleadings must be verified; deposits, the technology surcharge, and the security fee apply.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 4","t":"Court of Chancery: Process","s":"Covers summonses and service, including the statement required for consent-statute service (6 Del. C. §§ 15-114, 17-109, 18-109; 10 Del. C. § 3114) and seizure orders under 10 Del. C. § 365.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 5","t":"Court of Chancery: Service and Filing; Appearance and Withdrawal","s":"Sets electronic service and filing, the 5:00 p.m. and midnight deadlines, notices of discovery service, appearances, withdrawal, and redaction of personal identifiers.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 5.1","t":"Court of Chancery: Public Access; Confidential Filings","s":"Defines Confidential Information and sets the confidential filing, notice, designation, public-version, challenge, and 3-year expiration procedures.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 6","t":"Court of Chancery: Computing and Extending Time","s":"FRCP-style counting that excludes weekends and holidays for periods under 11 days, adds 3 days for mail-only service, and bars extending Rule 59 deadlines.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 7","t":"Court of Chancery: Pleadings; Motions; Briefs; Letters; Compendia","s":"Lists the permitted pleadings, requires answers to repeat each allegation, sets the motion, brief, and letter word limits, and authorizes compendia.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 10","t":"Court of Chancery: Caption; Form of Filings","s":"Sets the caption, typeface, spacing, margins, and Blue Book/Yellow Book citation rules, and requires a word-count certification in the signature block.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 11","t":"Court of Chancery: Signing Pleadings; Sanctions","s":"FRCP 11-style certification and sanctions; a sanctions motion is served but may not be filed if the challenged paper is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service (or another time the Court sets).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 12","t":"Court of Chancery: Defenses and Objections","s":"An answer is due within 20 days after service (10 days after the Court acts on a Rule 12 motion); a motion for a more definite statement is answered within 10 days and a motion to strike is due within 20 days; lists the Rule 12(b) defenses.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 15","t":"Court of Chancery: Amended and Supplemental Pleadings","s":"Amendment as of course or by leave; a blackline is required; Rule 15(a)(5) is the amend-or-stand rule (formerly 15(aa)); relation back uses a 120-day notice period.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 17","t":"Court of Chancery: Real Party in Interest; Capacity","s":"Real party in interest and capacity to sue (amended effective Sept. 25, 2023).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 23","t":"Court of Chancery: Class Actions","s":"Certification prerequisites and types, the 10-day representative affidavit, class counsel, Court-approved settlements and dismissals, and fee affidavits.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 23.1","t":"Court of Chancery: Derivative Actions","s":"Particularized pleading of demand efforts or excuse, standing, the 10-day affidavit, counsel selection, settlement approval, and fee affidavits.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 23.2","t":"Court of Chancery: Unincorporated Associations","s":"Suits by or against association members through a representative; requires Court approval of dismissals and settlements.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 40","t":"Court of Chancery: Call of Calendar; Continuances","s":"Annual calendar call to review delay; continuance motions for an absent witness need an affidavit.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 41","t":"Court of Chancery: Dismissal of Actions","s":"Voluntary dismissal is limited by Rules 15(a)(5), 23, and 23.1; covers involuntary dismissal and dismissal after 1 year of inaction.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 45","t":"Court of Chancery: Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas for testimony and documents; returns are e-filed as the “Subpoena” document type.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 54","t":"Court of Chancery: Judgment; Costs","s":"Defines judgment, provides for Rule 54(b) partial judgments, and awards costs to the prevailing party (rewritten effective June 1, 2026).","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 55","t":"Court of Chancery: Default; Default Judgment","s":"Default admits the allegations; 10 days’ notice of the application is required if the defaulting party has appeared.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 56","t":"Court of Chancery: Summary Judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 20 days; cross-motions without a disputed fact may be deemed a stipulation for decision on the record.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 57","t":"Court of Chancery: Declaratory Judgments","s":"Procedure under 10 Del. C. § 6501; the Court may order a speedy hearing.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 58","t":"Court of Chancery: Form of Judgment","s":"Except under Rule 54(b), a final order constitutes the judgment.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 59","t":"Court of Chancery: New Trial; Alter or Amend; Reargument","s":"10 days for a new trial or to alter or amend; 5 days for reargument or clarification; a timely motion suspends finality.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 60","t":"Court of Chancery: Corrections; Relief from Judgment","s":"Corrects clerical errors (with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed) and gives FRCP 60(b)-style relief; the text says only that a Rule 60(b) motion must be made “within a reasonable time” (no separate one-year cap appears), and the motion does not affect finality.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 62","t":"Court of Chancery: Stay Pending Appeal","s":"30-day automatic stay with exceptions; bonded stay of money judgments; Supreme Court Rules and Del. Const. art. IV, § 24 apply.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 65","t":"Court of Chancery: Injunctions and Restraining Orders","s":"Requires an affidavit or verified pleading; a TRO issued without notice lasts up to 10 days, extendable by 30; security is required.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 67","t":"Court of Chancery: Deposit in Court","s":"Funds go to a Delaware-present bank with at least $50 million in capital and surplus, in an interest-bearing account.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 69","t":"Court of Chancery: Execution","s":"Writs of execution in Superior Court form, executed by the sheriff, plus discovery in aid of execution.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 70","t":"Court of Chancery: Enforcing an Order for a Specific Act","s":"Substitute performance, vesting of title, writs, and contempt.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 72","t":"Court of Chancery: Appeals, Certifications and Mandates","s":"Motions to certify interlocutory appeals or questions of law to the Supreme Court; handling of the mandate.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 77","t":"Court of Chancery: Court and Register Open; Notice of Orders","s":"Court is always open for filing; lack of service of an order does not extend appeal time; remote trials are allowed.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 79","t":"Court of Chancery: The Docket","s":"Electronic docket and entries for judicial actions; Rules 79.1 and 79.2 (old e-filing rules) are omitted.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 81","t":"Court of Chancery: Corporate Election by Magistrate","s":"A Magistrate may run a director election, with a stockholder list 10 days before and notice at least 20 days before.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 83","t":"Court of Chancery: In Forma Pauperis","s":"Affidavit under 10 Del. C. §§ 8802–8804; after a denial, at least 15 days to pay.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 86","t":"Court of Chancery: Effective Date of Amendments","s":"Amendments apply to new cases and to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 88","t":"Court of Chancery: Expense Awards","s":"A motion to quantify a fee or expense award requires a detailed affidavit of rates, hours, and costs.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 90","t":"Court of Chancery: Civil Miscellaneous Access","s":"Guardianship and other C.M. matters are confidential; responses to access motions are due within 11 days.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 91","t":"Court of Chancery: Technology Disputes at Law","s":"Monetary-only technology disputes are heard only if the amount in controversy exceeds $1 million.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 92","t":"Court of Chancery: Consent to Litigate","s":"Prescribed consent language for 10 Del. C. § 346 technology litigation, with jury waiver.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 93","t":"Court of Chancery: Business and Technology Mediation","s":"Mediation under 10 Del. C. §§ 346–347; monetary-only claims must exceed $1 million.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 144","t":"Court of Chancery: Magistrate in Chancery Reports","s":"Reports, draft reports, de novo review, the 11-day/3-day exceptions schedules, and §§ 350–351 stipulations.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 170","t":"Court of Chancery: Attorneys","s":"Admission, pro hac vice motion by Delaware counsel, the applicant’s certification and assessment, and Delaware counsel’s duties.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 171","t":"Court of Chancery: Briefs","s":"Repealed effective Sept. 25, 2023; content transferred to Rules 7 and 10.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 171A","t":"Court of Chancery: CD-ROM Briefs","s":"Optional hyperlinked CD-ROM briefs filed within 10 days after the official brief.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 173","t":"Court of Chancery: Application for Argument or Trial; Emergencies","s":"Hearing requests go to the assigned judge; emergencies go first to the assigned judge, then to the Chancellor.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 174","t":"Court of Chancery: Mediation","s":"Court-annexed voluntary mediation before judicial or non-judicial mediators.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 178A","t":"Court of Chancery: Petition Contrary to Advance Health-Care Directive","s":"Revised effective June 1, 2026: verified petition, two physicians’ affidavits, and a 13-day notice and objection period.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"},{"n":"Ch. R. 180-B","t":"Court of Chancery: Powers and Duties of Guardian","s":"Revised effective June 1, 2026: covers Chapter 39A guardians; annual status report due the first business day of the appointment quarter.","u":"https://courts.delaware.gov/forms/download.aspx?id=304488"}]});
